.TH HMAP_IS_LOCATE 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
hmap_is_locate \- Check if a key is located in a hash map

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/hmap_internal.h>

.IB int
.IB hmap_is_locate(HMAP_DB* hmap_db, void* key, size_t k_len);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBhmap_is_locate\fR function checks if a key is located in a hash map. It takes a pointer to the hash map database, a key, and the length of the key as arguments.

The function takes the following parameters:
.IB HMAP_DB*
A pointer to the hash map database.
.IB void*
The key to check.
.IB size_t
The length of the key.

The function returns an integer indicating whether the key is located in the hash map. Possible return values include:
.IB HMAP_SUCCESS
The key is located in the hash map.
.IB HMAP_NOT_FOUND
The key is not located in the hash map.

The \fBhmap_is_locate\fR function is intended to be used to check if a key exists in a hash map.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBhmap_is_locate\fR function to check if a key is located in a hash map:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/hmap_internal.h>

int main() {
    HMAP_DB* hmap_db;
    // Initialize the hash map database
    hmap_init(&hmap_db);

    // Add a tuple with a string data type
    hmap_add_tuple_with_data(&hmap_db, "example1", 8, "data1");

    // Check if the key "example1" is located in the hash map
    int result = hmap_is_locate(hmap_db, "example1", 8);

    if(result == HMAP_SUCCESS) {
        printf("Key is located in the hash map\n");
    } else {
        printf("Key is not located in the hash map\n");
    }

    // Free the hash map database
    hmap_destroy(&hmap_db);

    return 0;
}
.nef